.TH DAG 1
.SH NAME
dag, Dag \- draw and edit a graph in dot
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dag
[
.B -e
]
.I file
.br
.B Dag
[
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Dag
draws a graph written in
.IR dot (1)'s
language, as
.I dot
lays it out, in a
.IR rio (1)
window.
The
.I file
is the graph's text, usually a node or an edge to a line:
.IP
.EX
digraph {
	a
	b
	a -> b
}
.EE
.PP
.B "dot -Txdot"
lays the graph out again after each change to the text.
If
.I file
does not exist, the graph starts empty, and
.B write
makes it.
.I Dag
needs the
.B graphviz
package, and adds it with
.IR pkg (1)
if
.B /bin/dot
is not there.
.PP
.I Dag
reads the text by
.IR dot 's
grammar, so a statement may span lines, a line may hold several, and comments, chains
.RB ( "a -> b -> c" ),
groups
.RB ( "a -> {b c}" )
and subgraphs are as
.I dot
reads them.
Each node and edge drawn is the text of its statements:
a node's are those that name it alone, or, if none does, the links of the edges it is in;
an edge's is its link in its statement, or, in a strict graph, every link that names its two nodes.
An edge of an undirected graph is told from its reverse as
.I dot
tells them.
Deleting an edge in a chain splits the chain there, and deleting a node in a group removes its name
from the group; the rest of the text stays as it was.
While
.I dot
runs, Escape stops it, and the line at the foot of
.IR dag 's
window says so.
.SS Mouse
.TF button\ 3
.TP
button 1
At nothing, adds a node's line before the graph's closing brace,
with the first free name of
.BR n1 ,
.BR n2 ,
.BR n3 ….
Dragged from a node to a node, adds an edge between them,
.B "a -> b"
in a digraph and
.B "a -- b"
in a graph; let go at nothing, it adds a new node too.
A click at a node selects it.
.I Dag
selects what it adds.
.TP
button 2
On a node or an edge, selects it; with Shift, adds it to the selection or takes it out.
Swept over nothing, selects what is inside the box.
A click at nothing selects nothing.
.TP
button 3
A menu:
.B Erase
erases what is selected, and a node's edges with it;
.B Undo
and
.BR Redo ;
.B Tred
sends the whole graph through
.IR tred ,
which removes the edges another path implies, and replaces the text with what it prints;
.B Write
writes the file;
.B Exit
ends
.IR dag .
.PD
.SS Keys
Delete and Backspace delete what is selected, and Escape selects nothing.
Command-Z undoes, Command-Shift-Z redoes, Command-A selects every node and edge,
Command-C copies the selection, as a graph of its own, to the snarf buffer, and Command-S writes the file.
.PP
Typing
.BR | ,
.B <
or
.B >
starts a command, shown at the bottom of the window; Return runs it and Escape drops it.
It runs
.IR rc (1)
in the file's directory.
A command works on the selection as a graph of its own, of the file's kind
.RB ( digraph
or
.BR graph ,
and
.B strict
if the file is), holding the selected nodes and edges with their attributes,
an edge of a chain or a group as an edge to itself:
.IP
.EX
digraph {
	c [color=red];
	a -> b;
}
.EE
.PP
.B |cmd
sends that graph to
.I cmd
and reads what it prints as the selection's new version:
what it no longer holds is deleted from the text as
.B delete
deletes it, what is new is added after the selection, and an attribute that changed is edited where it is.
Nothing else in the text changes.
A command that reorders lines, such as
.IR sort ,
may move the graph's first and last lines;
.I dag
takes them out wherever they are.
.B <cmd
reads what
.I cmd
prints in the same way, sending it nothing;
.B >cmd
sends the selection to
.IR cmd ,
whose output goes to standard error.
With nothing selected, the selection is an empty graph, so
.B |cmd
and
.B <cmd
add what
.I cmd
prints before the graph's closing brace, as
.IR acme (1)
puts a command's output at an empty dot.
What the command prints is always written, whatever its exit status, as
.IR acme (1)
writes it; its standard error and a failing status go to
.B +Errors
in
.IR Dag ,
and to standard error and the window's last line in
.IR dag .
If what it prints is not a graph,
.I dag
does what Acme would: it deletes the selection and puts the output where the selection's first statement was.
.PP
When the text does not parse, as after such output or an edit by hand,
.I dag
keeps the last graph it drew, says
.IR dot 's
error and its line, and makes no edits from the drawing until the text parses again;
Undo, in
.I dag
or in
.IR Dag 's
tag, brings the graph back.
.SS Atoms
With
.BR -e ,
.I dag
opens no window: it reads commands from standard input, a line each, does them, and exits.
.B node
.I name
and
.B edge
.I tail head
.RI [ n ]
select the nodes or edges so named (the
.IR n th
alone);
.B clear
selects nothing;
.BR delete ,
.BR add ,
which adds a node, and
.B add
.I tail head
do as the mouse does;
.B tred
is the menu's;
a line beginning with
.BR | ,
.B <
or
.B >
runs a command;
.B undo
undoes;
.B dot
prints the selection's dot, as
.I Dag
would set it, and
.B print
the text.
.SS Dag
.I Dag
is
.I dag
on an
.IR acme (1)
window, whose body is the graph's text.
With no
.IR file ,
it takes the window it runs in,
.BR $winid ;
with one, outside a window, or in a window whose
.B event
another program holds through
.IR amux (4),
as
.I win
does, it opens a new window on the file.
The window stays text, to edit as any other;
.I Dag
draws the graph in a
.IR rio (1)
window of its own, opened by
.I newwindow
(see
.IR graphics (2))
on
.B $wsys
before it reads or changes the Acme window, or says
.B "no $wsys"
and exits, and the window keeps the text window's name as it changes,
under a tag as Acme's
.RI ( adraw (2))
that holds the file's name,
.BR "Del Snarf |" ,
and
.BR "Undo Redo Put Erase Tred" .
An edit is one write of only the text it changes, guarded by the window's
.B seq
.RI ( acme (4)).
.BR Undo ,
.B Redo
and
.B Put
in
.IR Dag 's
tag, the menu's
.BR Undo ,
.B Redo
and
.BR Write ,
and Command-Z and Command-Shift-Z in the picture, write
.BR undo ,
.B redo
and
.B put
to the window's
.BR ctl ,
so they are Acme's;
.I Dag
draws the graph again after any change to the text, Undo's too, and its tag's square
shows whether the window has changes Put has not written.
If the body changed since
.I Dag
last read it, the edit is not made:
.I Dag
says the file changed, reads the body again, and leaves dot.
.I Dag
selects what dot covers when it starts, and sets dot as the selection changes:
to the selection's statements when they make one stretch of text,
and otherwise to an empty dot at the first of them.
.PP
In
.IR Dag 's
tag, a command beginning with
.BR | ,
.B <
or
.B >
is
.IR dag 's,
as if typed, and
.BR Erase ,
.B Tred
and
.B Exit
are the menu's.
A command beginning with
.BR | ,
.B <
or
.B >
runs while the picture goes on working, its name in the tag as Acme shows a running
command, and
.B Kill
stops it; one runs at a time, and its output is not used if the text changed while it ran.
A
.B >
command's output, its standard error and a failing status of its last command go to
.BR +Errors .
Other commands run as in an Acme tag, with
.B $winid
the text window and their errors in
.BR +Errors ;
.BR Dag ,
the tag's name for a window with no name, does nothing there.
.B Del
there, or
.BR Exit ,
ends
.I Dag
and closes its window; the text window stays.
Deleting the window in
.I rio
ends
.I Dag
too.
.PP
.I Dag
adds itself to the text window's tag, and
.B Dag
there ends it too.
.B Del
and
.B Delete
in that tag are Acme's own, by the window's
.B ctl
.RB ( del
and
.BR delete ):
.B Del
on a window with unsaved changes says so in
.B +Errors
and leaves it, and a second deletes it.
.I Dag
ends, and its window closes, when the text window is gone.
.B Edit
in the text window's tag stays Acme's, on dot: so
.B "Edit ,|tred"
sends the whole body through
.IR tred ,
and
.I Dag
draws the result.
.SH EXAMPLES
With part of a graph selected, remove the edges another path in that part implies:
.IP
.EX
|tred
.EE
.PP
Color the selected edges red; each one's attributes are edited where they are:
.IP
.EX
|gvpr -c 'E{color="red"}'
.EE
.PP
Remove repeated edges from the selection:
.IP
.EX
|sort -u
.EE
.PP
In the tag of the text window of a
.IR Dag ,
remove the nodes no edge leaves, in the whole graph:
.IP
.EX
Edit ,|gvpr -c 'N[outdegree==0]{delete(0,$)}'
.EE
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B src/cmd/dag
and, for Graphviz,
.BR mk/pkg
and
.B src/graphviz
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR dot (1),
.IR tred (1),
.IR gvpr (1),
.IR pkg (1),
.IR adraw (2),
.IR acme (4)
.SH BUGS
Only
.I dot
lays the graph out, and dragging a node does not move it.
.br
Text is drawn in the window's font, not the graph's.
.br
Acme keeps one
.B seq
guard and one
.B addr
for a window, so another program that writes them at the same moment, as
.IR hl
does as it paints, can take
.IR Dag 's
guard or move its address.
.br
A long edit is written in pieces, one Undo step; an edit from elsewhere between the pieces
leaves part of the rewrite in the body.
